technical_details:
Hi --

I believe I have found another remotely exploitable vulnerability in

the kernel. This one requires that NFSD is listening on port 2049. It

is a heap buffer overflow that allows for an adversary to write 944

bytes past the end of a buffer. I found this bug with the use of an

LLM, but have written this email myself and validated the bug myself.

I believe everything here is correct and any mistakes are mine.

The specific vulnerability is that when an NFSv4.0 LOCK operation is

denied due to a conflicting lock, the server encodes the conflicting

lock owner into a fixed-size 112-byte replay cache buffer without

bounds checking. A lock owner longer than ~80 bytes overflows the

buffer, corrupting adjacent kernel heap (slab) memory. An

unauthenticated network attacker with access to an NFSv4.0 server can

trigger this bug with two cooperating clients.

I've reproduced this vulnerability in v7.0-rc1 and in 6.18, but this

vulnerability is present as far back as 2.6 if I’m tracing things

properly.

NFSv4.0 uses a seqid-based replay cache for state-changing operations

(OPEN, LOCK, etc.). Each state owner (nfs4_stateowner) contains an

embedded nfs4_replay structure in /fs/nfsd/state.h with a fixed-size

buffer defined as follows:

#define NFSD4_REPLAY_ISIZE       112

struct nfs4_replay {

__be32 rp_status;

unsigned int rp_buflen;

char *rp_buf;

struct knfsd_fh rp_openfh;

int rp_locked;

char rp_ibuf[NFSD4_REPLAY_ISIZE];

};

There is a comment above the #define describing the reason for the value 112:

/* A reasonable value for REPLAY_ISIZE was estimated as follows:

 * The OPEN response, typically the largest, requires

 *   4(status) + 8(stateid) + 20(changeinfo) + 4(rflags) +  8(verifier) +

 *   4(deleg. type) + 8(deleg. stateid) + 4(deleg. recall flag) +

 *   20(deleg. space limit) + ~32(deleg. ace) = 112 bytes

 */

Unfortunately, this estimate only considered OPEN responses. It did

not account for LOCK denied responses, which include the conflicting

lock owner as a variable-length opaque field.

(A bit of fun history. This comment was correct as written — in fact,

the commit that sized this buffer wrote in the message "LOCK and

UNLOCK will be added when byte-range locking is done (soon!)”. But

when the LOCK code was added later, the size of this buffer was never

increased.)

The overflow vulnerability occurs in nfsd4_encode_operation(), which

both encodes a response and saves it to the replay cache in the same

call. It works in two phases.

1. Encode: Call the op-specific encoder (e.g., nfsd4_encode_lock()),

which writes the response into the XDR output stream. For a LOCK

denied, this calls nfsd4_encode_lock_denied(), which writes the full

lock4denied structure -- including the conflicting lock owner -- into

the XDR buffer.

2. Cache: Measure how many bytes were just encoded, then copy that

many bytes from the XDR stream into the replay cache buffer.

In the file fs/nfsd/nfs4xdr.c, the nfsd4_encode_operation function

works as follows:

void

nfsd4_encode_operation(struct nfsd4_compoundres *resp, struct nfsd4_op *op)

{

[…]

op_status_offset = xdr->buf->len;

[…]

op->status = encoder(resp, op->status, &op->u);

[…]

           if (so) {

int len = xdr->buf->len - (op_status_offset + XDR_UNIT);

so->so_replay.rp_status = op->status;

so->so_replay.rp_buflen = len;

read_bytes_from_xdr_buf(xdr->buf, op_status_offset + XDR_UNIT,

so->so_replay.rp_buf, len);

}

}

This final write is where the overflow happens, because len comes

(indirectly) from the adversary and is only constrained to be <= 1024

bytes in fs/nfsd/nfs4xdr.c comparing to NFS4_OPAQUE_LIMIT.

Reaching this line of code takes a bit of work. The attack requires

two cooperating NFSv4.0 clients and proceeds in 7 steps:

       Client A                    NFS Server                    Client B

         |                            |                            |

    (1)  |--- SETCLIENTID ----------->|                            |

         |<-- clientid_a, confirm ----|                            |

         |--- SETCLIENTID_CONFIRM --->|                            |

         |                            |                            |

    (2)  |--- OPEN "lockfile" ------->|                            |

         |<-- open_stateid_a ---------|                            |

         |--- OPEN_CONFIRM ---------->|                            |

         |                            |                            |

    (3)  |--- LOCK (1024-byte owner)->|  lock_owner = 1024b buf    |

         |<-- lock_stateid_a ---------|  Lock granted              |

         |                            |                            |

    (4)  |                            |<-- SETCLIENTID ------------|

         |                            |--- clientid_b, confirm --->|

         |                            |<-- SETCLIENTID_CONFIRM ----|

         |                            |                            |

    (5)  |                            |<-- OPEN "lockfile" --------|

         |                            |--- open_stateid_b -------->|

         |                            |<-- OPEN_CONFIRM -----------|

         |                            |                            |

    (6)  |                            |<-- LOCK (same range) ------|

         |                            |                            |

         |                   +--------+--------+                   |

         |                   | LOCK DENIED!    |                   |

         |                   | Encode response:|                   |

         |                   |   offset:    8B |                   |

         |                   |   length:    8B |                   |

         |                   |   type:      4B |                   |

         |                   |   clientid:  8B |                   |

         |                   |   owner_len: 4B |                   |

         |                   |   owner:  1024B |                   |

         |                   |   TOTAL:  1056B |                   |

         |                   |                 |                   |

         |                   | Copy to replay: |                   |

         |                   |   rp_ibuf[112]  |                   |

         |                   |   1056 > 112    |                   |

         |                   |  ** OVERFLOW ** |                   |

         |                   +--------+--------+                   |

         |                            |                            |

    (7)  |                            |--- NFS4ERR_DENIED -------->|

         |                            |                            |

         |  Kernel heap corrupted.    |                            |

         |  944 bytes overwritten     |                            |

         |  past rp_ibuf boundary.    |                            |

Note that the actual contents of the data on the wire are totally

normal. It is only the replay cache that has been corrupted.

I’ve validated this bug when running on a qemu image with both a 6.18

and 7.0 kernel. Below I’ve provided the KASAN crash where previously I

